# `actuarial::Operator<Signature>::operator_call`

[Back to `actuarial::Operator<Signature>`](../Operator.md)

Calls the stored callable.

## Signature

```cpp
Result actuarial::Operator<Signature>::operator()(Args... args) const;
```

## What It Does

Forwards the arguments to the stored callable and returns its result, with the types fixed by `Signature`. The call is `const`, so one operator can be shared. Whatever the callable throws propagates. Library operators are called the same way: a `Valuation` takes a `Cashflow`, a `LossTransform` takes a loss `Series`.

## Parameters

`args...`
: Arguments convertible to the parameter types of `Signature`.

## Returns

The callable's result.

## Errors

Throws `actuarial Operator is undefined` for an empty operator; exceptions from the callable propagate unchanged.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: a scalar call

Call the operator like the function it wraps.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Operator<double(double)> square = actuarial::make_operator<double(double)>("square", [](double x) { return x * x; });
  out.writeln(square(1.5), square(-2.0));
}
```

### Example 2: a vector call

One call applies a library operator to a whole series of losses.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series losses = pluss::make_series({50.0, 150.0, 400.0});
  out.writeln(actuarial::operators::deductible(100.0)(losses).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: composing calls

The result of one operator feeds the next.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Operator<double(double)> square = actuarial::make_operator<double(double)>("square", [](double x) { return x * x; });
  out.writeln(square(square(2.0)));
}
```

### Example 4: the errors

An empty operator and a throwing callable.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::Operator<double(double)>{}(1.0); });
  const auto strict = actuarial::make_operator<double(double)>("strict", [](double x) { if (x < 0) throw std::runtime_error("negative input"); return x; });
  report([&] { (void)strict(-1.0); });
}
```

